@node Embedding
@chapter Embedding

libdtfl is built to run inside a host program, such as a database
server, that supplies its own memory, error reporting, logging and
threads.  The five host hooks of @ref{Error handling} route each of
those boundaries through the host, and a byte transport lets the host
connect workers that live in separate processes.  This chapter shows the whole
embedding, using PostgreSQL as the example host.

@section Wiring the boundaries

A host installs the hooks once, before starting any computation.  Each
hook is a function the host provides plus an opaque context pointer.

@example
/*
 * The context libdtfl allocates in.  The hooks are installed once per
 * process, so they hold the address of this variable, not a context:
 * the host points it at the right context before each call into a
 * worker (see below).
 */
static MemoryContext dtfl_cxt;

static void *pg_malloc(size_t n, void *c)
@{ return MemoryContextAllocHuge(*(MemoryContext *)c, n); @}
static void *pg_realloc(void *p, size_t n, void *c)
@{ return p ? repalloc_huge(p, n)
           : MemoryContextAllocHuge(*(MemoryContext *)c, n); @}
static void pg_free(void *p, void *c) @{ pfree(p); @}

/* longjmp out of the computation; never returns */
static void pg_panic(const char *msg, void *c)
@{ ereport(ERROR, errmsg("dtfl: %s", msg)); @}

/* never elog(ERROR) here: see "After a panic" */
static void pg_log(dtfl_log_level lvl, const char *msg, void *c)
@{ int e[] = @{ DEBUG1, LOG, WARNING, LOG @}; elog(e[lvl], "%s", msg); @}

dtfl_cxt = AllocSetContextCreate(TopMemoryContext, "dtfl",
    ALLOCSET_DEFAULT_SIZES);
dtfl_alloc_hooks ah = @{ pg_malloc, pg_realloc, pg_free, &dtfl_cxt @};
dtfl_set_alloc_hooks(&ah);
dtfl_set_panic_hook(pg_panic, NULL);
dtfl_set_log_hook(pg_log, NULL);
/* no thread hooks: drive a standalone worker (below) */
@end example

Four details of the allocation hooks matter:

@itemize
@item
Use the @emph{huge} allocation functions.  @code{palloc} and
@code{repalloc} refuse requests over @code{MaxAllocSize} (1 GB less one
byte), and the columns of a large arrangement grow past that.
@code{MemoryContextAllocHuge} and @code{repalloc_huge} allow up to
@code{MaxAllocHugeSize}.
@item
@code{repalloc} and @code{pfree} need a pointer @code{palloc} returned;
@code{NULL} crashes the backend.  libdtfl never passes @code{NULL} to
@code{realloc_fn} or @code{free_fn}: it grows an empty buffer with
@code{malloc_fn} and skips freeing @code{NULL}.  The @code{p ?} test
above costs nothing and keeps the hook correct with older releases.
@item
Allocate in a dedicated context, not @code{CurrentMemoryContext}, and
reach it through a variable.  A query context is reset when the query
ends, while a persistent dataflow outlives it; and resetting the
worker's context is how the host discards a worker after an error.
Since the hooks are process-wide and set once, the variable is what
lets a host give each worker its own context: before any call that
builds, feeds or steps a worker, set @code{dtfl_cxt} to that worker's
context.  @code{repalloc} and @code{pfree} work on a chunk wherever it
was allocated, so only allocation follows the variable.  Create every
object a worker uses (time classes, diffs, record types, sessions,
probes) while its context is selected, and share none across workers
with different contexts.
@item
libdtfl's own objects need the alignment of @code{uint64_t},
@code{double} and pointers, which @code{palloc}'s @code{MAXALIGN}
provides.  Record and difference values are stored unaligned at
arbitrary offsets in any case, so callbacks read them with
@code{memcpy}; no allocator alignment changes that.
@item
The context belongs to libdtfl.  Allocate nothing of the host's own in
it, and make no child contexts under it: a reset after an error frees
them too.
@end itemize

The log levels map onto the host's own: @code{DTFL_LOG_DEBUG},
@code{DTFL_LOG_INFO}, @code{DTFL_LOG_WARN} and @code{DTFL_LOG_ERROR}
become @code{DEBUG1}, @code{LOG}, @code{WARNING} and @code{LOG}.  Not
@code{ERROR}: the log hook runs inside libdtfl, and an error raised there
unwinds through it (see below).  The panic hook is expected to
@code{longjmp} (via @code{ereport(ERROR)}) out of the computation rather
than return.

@section After a panic, discard the worker

A panic hook that @code{longjmp}s leaves the worker in an unknown state.
The panic can come from deep inside an operator, with a structure half
updated, a reference count not yet dropped, or a mutex held (queuing a
cross-thread activation allocates while holding one).  Rust is the same:
a panicking timely worker is not resumed.  So after the jump, nothing
reached from that worker may be used again: not its handles, not
@code{dtfl_worker_step}, not even @code{dtfl_worker_free}, which would
walk the inconsistent structures.

The host discards the worker by resetting its memory context with
@code{MemoryContextReset}, which frees every chunk and leaves the context
usable, and builds a new worker in it if it still needs the dataflow.
Do not delete the context instead unless the hook variable is pointed
at a live context before libdtfl allocates again: the hooks keep the
variable's address, and a deleted context's memory can be handed to
another context.  libdtfl's process-wide state (the hooks and their
latches, the per-thread error buffer) is not touched by the jump.

On Linux a standalone worker holds nothing but memory.  Elsewhere its
mutexes and condition variables may own small libc allocations that a
reset does not free, so each discarded worker leaks a little.  File
descriptors the host passed in (to @code{dtfl_event_writer_new} or the
replay functions) stay the host's to close.

The same holds for an @code{ereport(ERROR)} raised inside a callback
that libdtfl calls, such as an operator function, a sink or the log hook:
it unwinds through libdtfl's frames.  So the log hook maps
@code{DTFL_LOG_ERROR} to @code{LOG}, not @code{ERROR}, and a callback
that can fail catches its own error, records it, and returns normally;
the host checks the record after the step.

For a long-lived dataflow this means one error rebuilds everything that
worker held.  A host that keeps many views in one worker loses all of
them to one error, so keep independent dataflows in separate workers
(each with its own context), and make the rebuild path the same one used
after a restart.  Panics are contract violations and allocation failure;
validating input before it reaches libdtfl removes most of the first kind.

@section Hosts that control their own threads

A host that cannot hand libdtfl free-running threads has three ways to
embed it.

First, it can supply thread hooks whose @code{spawn} starts a thread
the host manages.  @code{dtfl_execute} then starts and joins each worker
through those hooks, and the rest of the program is unchanged.  The
hook must start a thread in the same process: the workers of one
@code{dtfl_execute} share memory.  A PostgreSQL background worker is a
separate process, so it cannot back these hooks, and a PostgreSQL
extension must not let libdtfl start threads at all, since
@code{palloc} and @code{ereport} are not thread-safe and every libdtfl
thread allocates through the hooks.  So an extension calls neither
@code{dtfl_execute} nor @code{dtfl_diag_server_start} (the diagnostics
server's thread), and uses the second or third way.

Second, and simplest when the host already has an event loop, it can
avoid @code{dtfl_execute} entirely and drive a single worker
cooperatively.  No thread is created, so no thread hook is needed; the
host calls @code{dtfl_worker_step} from its own thread whenever it
chooses, interleaving dataflow progress with its other work:

@example
dtfl_worker *w = dtfl_worker_new_standalone();
dtfl_worker_dataflow(w, "my", tc, build, ctx);
while (dtfl_worker_has_dataflows(w))
        dtfl_worker_step(w);
dtfl_worker_free(w);
@end example

Third, when the host already runs several processes (or tasks) and wants
them to share one computation, each joins it as one worker with
@code{dtfl_execute_worker(cfg, i, n, transport, fn, ctx)}, which runs
worker @var{i} of @var{n} on the calling thread and starts no thread.
The @code{dtfl_transport} is the host's: @code{send} and @code{recv}
move bytes to and from each peer without blocking, @code{wait} blocks
until a peer may have bytes or room or @code{wake} is called, and
@code{wake} may be called from any thread.  The worker's own steps move
the bytes, and it parks in @code{wait}.  @code{dtfl_transport_socket}
is a ready-made transport over connected sockets, such as a
@code{socketpair} mesh made before @code{fork}, and
@code{dtfl_config_set_workers(cfg, DTFL_WORKERS_PROCESSES)} makes
@code{dtfl_execute} build that mesh and fork the workers itself.
@file{examples/embed/forked_workers.c} shows the whole pattern.

@section PostgreSQL's concurrency models

@table @asis
@item Process per backend
The whole computation runs in one backend as a standalone worker
(the second way above), with the hooks of the previous sections.

@item Background workers
To spread one computation over several processes, the backend creates
a DSM segment with one @code{shm_mq} for each ordered pair of
participants, registers @var{n} - 1 dynamic background workers whose
@code{bgw_main_arg} names the segment and the worker's index, and joins
as worker 0 itself.  Every participant calls @code{dtfl_execute_worker}
with a transport whose @code{send} and @code{recv} are
@code{shm_mq_send} and @code{shm_mq_receive} with @code{nowait} set,
whose @code{wait} is @code{WaitLatch} on @code{MyLatch} followed by
@code{ResetLatch}, and whose @code{wake} is @code{SetLatch}.
@code{shm_mq} sets the peer's latch when it writes or reads, which is
exactly the condition @code{wait} must cover.  @code{dtfl_transport(3)}
gives the code.

@item AIO worker processes
With @code{io_method=worker} the postmaster forks I/O workers that run
a fixed loop over a shared submission ring, woken by @code{SetLatch}.
They host no libdtfl worker, and libdtfl submits no asynchronous I/O:
its spill files are read and written synchronously by the worker that
owns them.  The ring-plus-latch pattern is the same one a DSM-ring
transport uses, so a transport built for background workers works for
any process the postmaster starts.

@item A threaded backend
A server whose backends are threads (or fibers on a runtime such as
the libxtc-based multithreaded branch) can let libdtfl run several
workers in one address space.  The thread hooks spawn them as the
server's own threads or tasks, and @code{dtfl_set_sync_hooks} maps
libdtfl's mutexes, condition variables, the word wait and wake it
parks on, yield, sleep and the clock onto the server's primitives, so a
parked worker yields its carrier instead of sleeping in the kernel.
@code{palloc} must then be safe on those threads, which is the server's
contract, not libdtfl's.
@end table

@section A fiber runtime

A runtime whose tasks are fibers, such as libxtc, needs every wait to
go through it.  @code{dtfl_set_sync_hooks} does that for locks, the
parker, yield, sleep and the clock: an asynchronous mutex and condition
variable are enough, since the parker builds on the condition when no
@code{wait}/@code{wake} pair is given, and @code{self} should name the
fiber, not its carrier, so the debug owner check survives migration.
The thread hooks spawn tasks.  To keep sockets out of the picture too,
each task runs @code{dtfl_execute_worker} over an in-memory transport
built on the runtime's channels, whose @code{wait} parks the fiber and
whose @code{wake} wakes it.

@section What libdtfl never does

libdtfl installs no signal handlers and calls no @code{getenv},
@code{signal} or @code{getopt}.  When a host wants environment lookup, it
passes a callback to @code{dtfl_config_from_env}; the library never
reads the environment itself.  This keeps libdtfl safe to load into a
host that owns those facilities.  The complete contract, including the
per-thread ownership rules, is in @code{dtfl_embedding(7)} and
@code{dtfl(7)}.
